== KEY DESIGN RULES ==

1. Repository pattern is mandatory. Every feature defines an abstract repository
   in domain/repositories. The concrete impl in data/repositories composes a
   remote datasource (Dio) and a local datasource (storage). Providers and UI
   depend ONLY on the abstract repository, never on Dio or storage directly.

2. Realtime abstraction. Define `RealtimeSource<T>` in core/realtime exposing a
   `Stream<T>` (or stream of change-events) for a resource. Implement
   `PollingSource` now (calls the repository every N seconds, emits results).
   The repository/provider consumes a RealtimeSource via DI, so switching to
   SSE/sockets later means adding a new RealtimeSource impl and changing one
   provider override — nothing else.

3. Storage abstraction. Define `KeyValueStore` (or a typed cache interface) in
   core/storage with an in-memory implementation now. Local datasources depend
   on this interface so a real local DB (e.g. drift/sqlite/isar) can replace
   the memory impl later without touching repositories.

4. Sync seam. Add a `SyncEngine` interface in core/sync with a no-op
   implementation now, wired but inert, so a real sync layer slots in later.

5. State management. Use Riverpod with code generation (@riverpod). Use
   AsyncNotifier for async/server-backed state. All dependencies (api client,
   repositories, sources, stores) are exposed as providers so they can be
   overridden in tests and in main.dart. No global singletons.

6. Models immutable via freezed. DTOs in data layer, entities in domain layer;
   map between them in the repository (don't leak DTOs to the UI).

7. Error handling via the Result type; map Dio errors to ApiException in the
   api client; no raw exceptions leaking to the UI.

